WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:06.105
People say the command line for three different things.

2
00:00:06.105 --> 00:00:07.760
The terminal is the window.

3
00:00:07.933 --> 00:00:10.474
The shell is the program running inside it.

4
00:00:10.474 --> 00:00:13.664
It reads the line you type, and starts other programs.

5
00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:17.621
And a command is usually a program file on disk.

6
00:00:17.621 --> 00:00:19.771
ls is the file /usr/bin/ls.

7
00:00:19.771 --> 00:00:23.911
The shell finds it by searching the folders in PATH.

8
00:00:24.667 --> 00:00:28.056
Here is what happens between Enter and the program.

9
00:00:28.056 --> 00:00:29.053
Take this line.

10
00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:32.547
Before any program starts, the shell takes it apart.

11
00:00:32.547 --> 00:00:35.187
It splits the line on spaces, into words.

12
00:00:35.333 --> 00:00:37.900
The first word is the program to run.

13
00:00:38.067 --> 00:00:43.065
Every other word becomes an argument: a plain string handed to that program.

14
00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:47.831
You know this shape from Kotlin: fun main, with args, an array of strings.

15
00:00:47.831 --> 00:00:52.024
The shell builds args, and the program decides what each one means.

16
00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:57.218
By habit, arguments that start with a dash are options, also called flags.

17
00:00:57.218 --> 00:00:59.659
The last one is a folder to work on.

18
00:01:00.933 --> 00:01:06.307
Because spaces split words, a file name with a space in it causes trouble.

19
00:01:06.307 --> 00:01:11.827
ls my notes.txt asks for two files, my and notes.txt, and prints two errors.

20
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.404
Quote the name, and it stays one word.

21
00:01:14.404 --> 00:01:18.136
Better still, keep spaces out of the file names you create.

22
00:01:18.867 --> 00:01:21.367
Now a wildcard, also called a glob.

23
00:01:21.367 --> 00:01:23.866
In the lab, type echo star dot log.

24
00:01:23.866 --> 00:01:26.652
The star matches any run of characters.

25
00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:29.230
The shell splits the line, as before.

26
00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:30.675
But it sees a pattern.

27
00:01:30.867 --> 00:01:35.865
Before the program starts, the shell reads the folder, and keeps the names that fit.

28
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.100
Then it puts them, sorted, in place of the pattern.

29
00:01:39.267 --> 00:01:42.482
The program never sees the pattern, only the names.

30
00:01:42.482 --> 00:01:45.445
And echo just prints the arguments it receives.

31
00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:47.363
That makes echo a good preview.

32
00:01:47.363 --> 00:01:51.971
Put it in front of a risky command, and it prints the line the program would get.

33
00:01:52.667 --> 00:01:54.399
Quotes switch globbing off.

34
00:01:54.399 --> 00:01:58.120
echo, with star dot log in quotes, prints the star itself.

35
00:01:58.267 --> 00:02:00.070
And when nothing matches?

36
00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:02.018
Here bash and zsh disagree.

37
00:02:02.018 --> 00:02:07.140
In bash, the pattern passes on unchanged, so echo prints star dot text.

38
00:02:07.333 --> 00:02:10.201
In zsh, the shell refuses to run the line.

39
00:02:10.201 --> 00:02:15.048
You will see the zsh way on your Mac, and the bash way on every server.

40
00:02:15.733 --> 00:02:17.918
Now the most common mistake.

41
00:02:17.918 --> 00:02:19.947
One stray space is enough.

42
00:02:19.947 --> 00:02:22.132
Say you type rm -r old logs.

43
00:02:22.267 --> 00:02:24.510
The shell splits on that space.

44
00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:27.187
So rm gets two folders, old and logs.

45
00:02:27.333 --> 00:02:28.549
It deletes both.

46
00:02:28.549 --> 00:02:32.957
rm has no trash and no undo, and nothing brings them back.

47
00:02:33.133 --> 00:02:38.943
So before any delete, run pwd, preview the line with echo, and read it once more.

48
00:02:39.667 --> 00:02:43.707
So the shell rewrites your line before the program starts.

49
00:02:43.707 --> 00:02:48.931
It splits on spaces, expands the wildcards, and only then runs the program.

50
00:02:48.931 --> 00:02:50.951
rm only ever sees file names.
